\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah Ends Up On Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2001\par 10:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The rupiah recouped early losses to end slightly higher Friday on suspected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $10 million Friday to help the local unit, which was depressed by dollar demand from local companies. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 10,500 rupiah, down from its close Thursday at IDR10,560. The dollar had risen to an intraday high of IDR10,590 shortly after interbank trading opened and before Bank Indonesia was suspected to have flexed its muscles in the market. \par \par \uc2  "Unlike Thursday, Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today was successful thanks to the thin trading volume," said a dealer with a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't comment on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although officials have frequently stated that it will defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar demand will continue ahead of the end of the year as local companies have to pay maturing offshore debts and multinational companies here send profits back to their home countries. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia is expected to remain the largest dollar supplier in the market due to the absence of foreign capital inflows, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is expected to trade between IDR10,400 and IDR10,600 next week. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sen