\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah Ends Up On Pft-Taking,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Talk \b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2001\par 10:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah rebounded against the dollar in volatile trade Tuesday on profit-taking, spurred by talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after the local unit fell to a new three-month low earlier in the day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 10,835 rupiah, down from its close Monday at IDR10,940. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly touched IDR11,000 earlier in the day, a level unseen since late July. \par \par \uc2  However, suspected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  along with profit-taking on the dollar rescued the battered local unit, dealers said. They suspect that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold between $15 million and $20 million Tuesday. Bank Indonesia's officials weren't available for immediate confirmation. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide below IDR10,900 prompted some market participants, who were still holding long dollar positions to sell their dollars back at loss. These dragged the dollar to an intraday low of IDR10,720 before fresh bids by local companies pushed it higher. \par \par \uc2  "We sold around $20 million today (Tuesday) to take profit as the market has been overly long on the dollar," said a dealer with a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Underlying sentiment on the rupiah, however, remains bearish as the market doesn't foresee much improvement in Indonesia's economy or investment climate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The market will closely watch international creditors' two-day meeting commencing Wednesday in Jakarta. They are expected to pledge new loans of between $3 billion and $4 billion to Indonesia to help finance the 2002 state budget. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to trade between IDR10,750 and IDR10,900 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011106dxb6000k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
