\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Indonesian rupiah hits 11,000/dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Chen \par \uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2001\par 07:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 6 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah slipped past the 11,000 to the dollar mark on Tuesday for the first time since July 23 on corporate dollar demand as investors stayed pessimistic about Indonesia's economy, dealers in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  At 0548 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at 10,820/10,850, compared with 10,860 late on Monday. It touched the 11,000 mark earlier in the day, though profit-taking on the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rescued it from further losses. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted selling dollars aggressively in the market during the morning session, catching the market long on dollar, dealers said. A dealer at a foreign bank in Jakarta estimated the bank sold around $20 million, which he said was an unusually large amount. \par \par \uc2  Still, the dollar was well-supported around these levels, with local corporates including investment houses looking to buy it on dips. \par \par \uc2  Daily charts showed the dollar/rupiah was still overbought, though less so than it was this morning. The pair now has a 14-day relative strength index reading of 86, compared with 94 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  However, given Indonesia's poor economic fundamentals and the dearth of fresh dollar inflows, the rupiah is unlikely to enjoy a long-lasting rebound, no matter how overbought the dollar may appear. \par \par \uc2  GOOD NEWS FOR INDONESIA \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said players were hoping to reach the 11,500 to the dollar mark before taking profit on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  However, there was a rare bit of good news for Indonesia. The IMF said it would work as quickly as possible to sign a new pact with Jakarta. The country's IMF representative David Nellor said the Fund was impressed by the progress in some reforms. \par \par \uc2  But the market shrugged off the comments, with players more concerned about the immediate dearth of dollar inflows. \par \par \uc2  A team from the International Monetary Fund will also arrive this week to review current reforms, and begin talks on a new letter of intent under its $5 billion loan programme. \par \par \uc2  The chronically ailing rupiah has been falling steadily since mid-August, when optimism over President Megawati Sukarnoputri's new government began to fade. It has given up nearly all its gains from July 23-August 14, when it rallied from 11,125 to 8,200 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was upset by signs of further trouble with the country's donors and lenders, with whom Jakarta needs good relations to help keep the struggling economy afloat. \par \par \uc2  The market was particularly perturbed by a critical report from the World Bank issued on Monday and by Standard and Poor's decision over the weekend to downgrade Indonesia's sovereign ratings to CCC from CCC-Plus, with a negative outlook. \par \par \uc2  PARIS CLUB MEET \par \par \uc2  Both reports came just ahead of key meetings between Jakarta and its donor and lenders. Jakarta will hold talks with its Consultative Group of donors on Wednesday and Thursday, during which the government is expected to ask for $3 billion to $4 billion in aid. \par \par \uc2  Next week, Indonesia will meet with its Paris Club of official lenders, and is expected to seek a restructuring of some $6 billion in debt. \par \par \uc2  The World Bank report said it expected the government to still "muddle through" the country's economic woes, adding that it needed to move more quickly to ensure fiscal sustainability and improve the investment climate. \par \par \uc2  Japan, the country's biggest creditor nation, has added its voice to the growing criticism over Jakarta's foot-dragging on reforms. The Japanese government said on Monday it wanted to see an improvement in reform efforts before disbursing fresh loans and grants. \par \par \uc2  Few expect Indonesia's donors and lenders to turn their backs on the country given its geo-political importance in the region's stability and the fact that it is the world's most populous Muslim nation. \par \par \uc2  Still, patience has been wearing thin over the snail-like pace on reform. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the focal point of the meetings will be Jakarta's fumbling of asset sales and the privatisation process. \par \par \uc2  SEMEN GRESIK \par \par \uc2  The recent takeover by the West Sumatra provincial government of a unit of cement company PT Semen Gresik to prevent its sale to Mexico-based Cemex highlights the government's failure to raise revenues through asset sales to help fund the budget. \par \par \uc2  Though the government has yet to raise one cent towards its 2001 target of 6.5 trillion rupiah in asset sales, it has not revised its target yet. \par \par \uc2  State Enterprises Minister Laksama Sukardi recently conceded the government may have to re-think the timetable for its target after persistently sticking by it, saying the government had little choice given the budget deficit. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011106dxb6032tb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
