\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Indonesia says no immediate target for rupiah. \b0\par\par\uc2 162 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2001\par 07:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 5 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smooth out sharp fluctuations in the rupiah but said it had no immediate target for the brittle \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Senior deputy bank governor Anwar Nasution said the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves could not be used excessively. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah weakened further early on Monday, approaching 11,000 to the dollar, having lost nearly all its gains since the day President Megawati Sukarnoputri took power in late July. By 0600 GMT it was being quoted at 10,850/10,900 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "(We) will keep doing it (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) but cannot use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves excessively," Nasution told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  When asked if there was any plan to prevent the rupiah from hitting 11,000, Nasution added: "There is no such target. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that we do is aimed at preventing drastic fluctuations. That's it." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011105dxb500bl6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
