\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Rupiah Ends Sharply Lower On US Attacks \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2001\par 12:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The rupiah took a beating in volatile trade Monday on market concerns that the U.S.-led air strike against Afghanistan will provoke violent anti-U.S. protests in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped at its open to its intraday high of 10,500 rupiah, a new ten-week high. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking and alleged Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drove the dollar lower to close at IDR10,163, 3.3% above its IDR9,825 close on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The militant Islamic Defenders Front has repeatedly threatened to attack U.S. citizens and interests in Indonesia if there are military strikes against Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  The group has also threatened to assassinate U.S. Ambassador Robert Gelbard. \par \par \uc2  However, as of late Monday only around 600 students were reported to have staged a peaceful protest outside Gelbard's residence. They earlier protested outside the U.S. and U.K. embassies. \par \par \uc2  Police deployed dozens of armored vehicles and hundreds of personnel to prevent violent street protests. The U.S. embassy told its citizens living in Indonesia to stay at home in case of violent protests. Other western embassies gave similar advice to their citizens. \par \par \uc2  Investors are worried that the Indonesian government may need to crackdown on unruly radical Muslim groups, which could escalate into wider opposition if such a crackdown turns bloody. \par \par \uc2  "Things will become more complicated if the government mishandles the radical Muslim problem," said a dealer with a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  The government has urged Indonesians not to overreact to the attacks on Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  Still, the market is worried that more anti-U.S. protests may take place in coming days and the concern remains that these will turn violent, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  In a move to calm the market, Bank Indonesia Governor Sjahrir Sabirin reiterated Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to sell dollars to defend the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold dollars recently, but he declined say if it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold between $10 million and $15 million in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to test IDR10,500 again Tuesday as they expect the anti-U.S. protests to grow. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011008dxa8000p3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
