\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late, Rupiah Leads Fall \b0\par\par\uc2  By Irene Kwek \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2001\par 12:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were lower Monday, with the rupiah diving amid concerns that a series of air strikes against military installations in Afghanistan could spark further anti-U.S. protests in Indonesia, the world's most populous Muslim nation. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, the reaction in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was somewhat subdued, as a holiday in Tokyo sapped volume. Bucking the trend, the New Taiwan dollar and the South Korean won ended slightly higher as local players reduced their positions in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Thio Chin Loo, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BNP Paribas, noted that the dollar performed better Monday than in the immediate aftermath of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. She said this was partly because the U.S. and its allies had been telegraphing the retaliatory strikes for weeks, giving investors a chance to adjust positions. \par \par \uc2  "Prior to the (Sept. 11) event, the market was overall very bullish on the dollar, and post that event, the market has scaled back on their long dollar positions," she said. \par \par \uc2  Late Sunday, U.S. and U.K. forces began a series of bombing assaults on terrorist bases and other military sites in Afghanistan in an effort to destroy the terrorist organization run by Osama bin Laden, who is thought to have masterminded the Sept. 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  Fears that the conflict could escalate prompted a wave of safe-haven flows into the Swiss franc Monday - and a flight out of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen most vulnerable to heightened tension, such as the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Highlighting the risks in Jakarta, one hardline group, the Islamic Defenders Front, responded to the bombing raids by publicly threatening to round up and expel U.S. citizens and other foreigners if Indonesia doesn't break diplomatic ties with Washington. \par \par \uc2  "We will destroy American assets and attack the U.S. Embassy," said Al Habib Muhammad Riziq Syihab, who heads the small but vocal anti-U.S. group. \par \par \uc2  At one point Monday, concerns about the potential for instability in Indonesia drove the rupiah down by 6% against the dollar, pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above IDR10,500. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped put a floor under the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the dollar closed at IDR10,160, up from IDR9,830 late Friday. Dealers said they believed Bank Indonesia sold $5 million to stem losses in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia Deputy Governor Miranda Gultom said the volatility in the rupiah was temporary and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to sell dollars as needed. However, traders pointed out that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited reserves to prop up the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Asia, Mansoor Mohi-uddin, expects the dollar to trade in a range of IDR9,500 to IDR10,500 during the next few days. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished higher against the Singapore dollar, rising to S$1.7939 from S$1.7838 late Friday. The dollar rose as high as S$1.7960, a two-month high, before drifting back down. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a large local bank was among the big dollar buyers - apparently for commercial purposes. Also, a U.S. investment house was reportedly buying Australian dollars and selling Singapore dollars, possibly in anticipation of Singapore Airlines Ltd. (P.SAL) making a bid for Ansett, the failed Australian airline company. \par \par \uc2  In the next few sessions, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely be supported at S$1.7880 and face key resistance at S$1.8000, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, the peso closed slightly lower in range-bound trade, with the dollar closing at its intraday high of PHP51.600, compared with PHP51.565 Friday. \par \par \uc2  After the close of trading, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Rafael Buenaventura, denied rumors in the market that the bank had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the peso. Buenaventura also expressed hope that the peso wouldn't be hurt by news of several bombings in the southern part of the country early Monday. \par \par \uc2  Members of the extremist Muslim group Abu Sayyaf, which has ties to Osama bin Laden, are suspected of carrying out a series of bombings in three hotels in the province of Zamboanga in southern Philippines, a police official said. \par \par \uc2  Buenaventura downplayed the impact of the domestic attacks on peso sentiment, saying, "The market is taking it in stride." \par \par \uc2  In Bangkok, the dollar was at THB44.800 late Monday, up from THB44.705, buoyed by its gains against other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders said resistance was pegged at THB44.850. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended slightly higher at NT$34.557 to the dollar, compared with NT$34.559, in light trade, as many players retreated to the sidelines due to increased uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  In contrast to \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday to prevent the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too quickly, purchasing around US$100 million in exchange for New Taiwan dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Mohi-uddin from UBS Warburg said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was controlling the market very closely. "If you look at the September \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  numbers - at an all time high in September - it shows how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying." He added this will likely continue in October. \par \par \uc2  Monetary officials said Monday that Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a record US$115.20 billion at the end of September from US$114.14 billion in August. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the South Korean won finished slightly stronger, as the dollar dipped to KRW1,311.6 from KRW1,312.2. \par \par \uc2  -By Irene Kwek, Dow Jones Newswires; +(65) 4154 134; irene.kwek@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011008dxa8000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
