\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Rupiah Ends Higher;Corporate Dlr Demand Fizzles \b0\par\par\uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2001\par 13:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended higher Thursday as market participants unwound long dollar positions after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to sustain gains above the 9,900 rupiah level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 9,738 rupiah, down from its close at IDR9,915 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell below IDR9,900 in early morning trade and, although it briefly recovered to touch that level midday, failed to sustain its gains as fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the U.S. unit lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But by Thursday dollar demand from local companies evaporated whenever the dollar rose past IDR9,850, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We reversed our positions as dollar demand fizzled above IDR9,850," said a dealer with a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the rupiah, although hasn't provided a target level for the local unit. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah had weakened beyond the psychologically important IDR10,000 level Tuesday to hit a three-month low against the dollar, although it regained some ground Wednesday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A dealer with a state-owned bank said market participants saw the interest rate differential as another reason to unload dollars. "It's really costly to maintain long positions in the dollar with the (one-month) rupiah interest rate at around 17.8%, compared to the dollar interest rate at 2.5%," he said. \par \par \uc2  News that the International Monetary Fund doesn't object to the government's plan to sell a 51% stake in PT Bank Central Asia in tranches further helped dissipate bearish sentiment on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency said Tuesday that it would sell a 30% stake in BCA before year-end, while the remaining 21% will be sold later. Analysts said that appeared to break a key agreement with the IMF to sell the entire 51% stake before the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  However the IMF's senior representative in Indonesia, David Nellor, told Dow Jones Newswires: "at this stage they (the Indonesian government) have the discretion to sell 51% and will make a judgment at the appropriate time on commercial considerations, and what's best for the country." \par \par \uc2  Still, dealers said the rupiah is susceptible to weakness if bad news catches the market by surprise. \par \par \uc2  They expect the dollar to trade between IDR9,675 and IDR9,850 Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011004dxa4000q5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
