\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian Rupiah Ends Dn; \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fails To Stem Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 11:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah closed lower Monday as strong dollar demand persisted and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to stem the dollar's rise, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted between IDR9,310 and IDR9,340 late Monday, compared with IDR9,100 to IDR9,130 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said demand for dollars remains strong as state-owned companies continue to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for debt repayments and to cover imports. \par \par \uc2  They said Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with around $20 million to stop the rupiah's slide, but with little success. \par \par \uc2  Traders now expect the rupiah to fall towards IDR9,500 this week on sustained dollar demand from state-owned companies. \par \par \uc2  "We think that Bank Indonesia cannot do anything because the demand is huge," said one dealer with a Jakarta-based foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar was traded in a wide range of IDR9,100 to IDR9,340 Monday and could hit IDR9,400 Tuesday, although profit-taking is likely to set in. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the dollar to trade in a IDR9,300 to IDR9,400 range Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Leigh Murray, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277;\par leigh.murray@dowjones.com\par -0- 17/09/01 09-29G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917dx9h02iqg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
