\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian Rupiah Ends Lower As Local Companies Buy Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2001\par 11:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah closed lower Tuesday as local companies bought U.S. dollars to repay maturing offshore debts, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah, however, came off its intraday low due to dollar selling by state banks in late trading. \par \par \uc2  Dealers believed state banks were selling dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local unit. \par \par \uc2  After hitting an intraday high of 9,140 rupiah, the dollar closed at IDR9,045, up from its close of IDR8,970 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar selling by the state banks, dealers expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain well supported by demand from local companies in the coming days. \par \par \uc2  Dollar purchases to finance imports by local companies are also expected to buoy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Dollar purchase for debt repayment will continue to support the dollar this week," said a dealer with a foreign bank. "Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only provide a temporary boost to the rupiah." \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to trade between IDR8,900 and IDR9,100 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010904dx94000kw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
