\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Rupiah Ends Weaker As Cos Buy Dollars For Debt \b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2001\par 11:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah ended weaker Wednesday as local companies bought dollars to service month-end foreign debt repayments, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling about $30 million to try to stop the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, traders said, but this wasn't enough to stop it reaching an intraday high of IDR9,200. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed Asian trade at IDR9,175, much higher than IDR8,970 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is desperate for the local unit to maintain the gains it has made since President Megawati Sukarnoputri came to power July 23. After the transition the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered from year lows of more than IDR12,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to return to these levels now that Indonesia's political crisis is over, demand for dollars from local companies to repay debt continues to weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said local companies, including state-owned oil company Pertamina, bought dollars to fund foreign repayments. Pertamina was also buying dollars to cover petroleum imports. \par \par \uc2  They expect Bank Indonesia to continue to protect the rupiah Thursday, with the dollar expected to trade between IDR9,100 and IDR9,250. \par \par \uc2  "Until the month ends I think the demand for dollars will be high," said one foreign bank trader. \par \par \uc2  The weighted average interest rate on one-month Sertifikat Bank Indonesia notes rose slightly Wednesday despite the International Monetary Fund's decision to allow Indonesia to loosen its monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The one-month SBI notes stood at 17.67% at the weekly auction, up from 17.61% last week. \par \par \uc2  -By Leigh Murray, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; leigh.murray@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010829dx8t000jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
