\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Firm yen helps, U.S. worries remain. \b0\par\par\uc2 989 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2001\par 11:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late on Friday as the yen's resilience offered support in some quarters while corporate dollar demand weighed elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  enjoyed broad support, thanks to selling of the euro by local exporters and investors frustrated by its failure to rise. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also failed to make much headway against the yen despite further jawboning by Japanese officials, with the Finance Ministry's Haruhiko Kuroda saying recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves did not reflect fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Overnight news that U.S. unemployment claims rose 8,000 last week and the number of people remaining on the unemployment rolls reached near nine-year highs only added to pessimism over the likely timing of a U.S. recovery. \par \par \uc2  In Asia, the Singapore and Taiwan dollars took heart from the firm yen while the Thai baht reached near five-month highs as players cut long dollar positions ahead of the imposition of new \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules on September 3. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won was held back by importer dollar buying and a perception the authorities did not want to see it strengthen further. The Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso gave way to commercial demand for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht racked up healthy gains as offshore parties ditched long dollar positions before new \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  disclosure rules take effect on September 3, with major U.S. names seen selling actively. \par \par \uc2  The baht was also underpinned by a firmer yen and some other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the market was cautious not to push the baht up too fast, fearing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in to buy the dollar on dips to repay IMF debts. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said on Friday his government would try to stimulate economic growth to above two percent in 2001. \par \par \uc2  His remark came after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Pridiyathorn Devakula scaled down the 2001 growth forecast to 1.8 percent from 2.0-3.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar rode the yen and baht's coat-tails as interbank players cut long dollar positions ahead of the weekend and ventured into some cross plays. \par \par \uc2  But gains were capped by corporate demand for the dollar at around 1.7500, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar was also weighed by fears that SingTel's deal to buy Australia's second-largest telecoms company could lead to outflows, but analysts said funding for the deal should already be covered. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's consumer price index rose 1.3 percent in July against a year earlier and was up 0.6 percent from June. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah traded around the lower end of its recent range due to local commercial dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said it moved in a 8,520-8,720 range, with one oil company spotted buying dollars actively. \par \par \uc2  Energy ministry officials said Indonesia's crude oil output, the country's major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earner, was expected to fall by an average 100,000 barrels per day next year due to security disruptions in fields. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in the rupiah interbank money market unchanged at 15.125 for the overnight rate and 15.875 percent for the seven-day rate. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso closed at 51.05 in the onshore market, just above its intraday low as players bought dollars to settle non-deliverable forward contracts. \par \par \uc2  Trading was active, with $107.50 million transacted compared with only $62.30 million on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was also dampened by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rafael Buenaventura's remarks on Thursday that the bank was not currently considering further measures to support the peso. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won closed at 1,281.2 - at the higher end of its recent 1,280-1,290 range - thanks to the yen's gains. \par \par \uc2  But Seoul dealers said trading was light given the dearth of domestic news. Indeed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has largely remained within the 1,280-1,290 range for the whole week. \par \par \uc2  The won has been tracking the yen closely as Korea and Japan compete on a number of exports. Singapore dealers said the market has recently been too timid to test the won's upside due to wariness over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities. \par \par \uc2  Korean officials have verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the won's rise to maintain export competitiveness. Last week, a state bank was spotted buying dollars when the won rose past 1,280, believed to be on behalf of the authorities. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar closed marginally firmer on Friday, supported by the yen and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But its upside was capped by fears of capital outflows as Taipei stocks closed at four-week lows on weak corporate earnings and uncertainty over a weekend economic committee meeting aimed at reviving the battered economy. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were net sellers of T$3.65 billion (US$105 million) of local shares on Friday, bringing total net selling to T$9.65 billion over a three-session selling streak. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0905 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     119.83       120.31         +0.40\par  Sing dlr      1.7490       1.7555         +0.37\par  Taiwan dlr    34.625       34.629         +0.01\par  Korean won   1281.00      1279.00         -0.16\par  Baht           44.29        44.62         +0.75\par  Peso           51.10        51.00         -0.20\par  Rupiah       8710.00      8700.00         -0.11\par  Ringgit       3.7998       3.7995         -0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.83       114.45         -4.49\par  Sing dlr      1.7490       1.7365         -0.71\par  Taiwan dlr    34.625       32.900         -4.98\par  Korean won   1281.00      1277.00         -0.31\par  Baht           44.29        43.70         -1.33\par  Peso           51.10        50.75         -0.68\par  Rupiah       8710.00      9470.00         +8.73\par  Ringgit       3.7998       3.7995         -0.01.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010824dx8o00fa7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
