\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Mixed, glimmer of hope in yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2001\par 03:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 20 (Reuters) - Asian currrencies gave mixed signals early Friday, despite the weaker dollar. \par \par \uc2  While the firmer yen was likely to pull the North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, the Singapore dollar was still struggling under commercial demand for the U.S. unit. The Philippine peso was also expected to come under pressure with import season underway. \par \par \uc2  And the Indonesian rupiah was floundering after President Abdurrahman Wahid warned parliament on Thursday of further violence from his supporters if efforts to oust him continued. \par \par \uc2  U.S. markets' wildly differing reactions to the Federal Reserve's recent surprise rate cuts left investors in Asia scratching their heads over what exactly the growth prospects for the U.S. and the region were. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street was gleeful about the cuts, taking them as proof that the Fed was pulling out the stops to avoid a recession, but Treasuries despaired that the economy was on the path towards overheating and rising inflation. \par \par \uc2  And \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets agonised as to whether the Fed's move meant the U.S. economy was in worse shape than previously thought, leaving the dollar limp against the yen and euro . \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won edged lower from its Thursday close of 1,298, though a firmer yen helped limit its fall. \par \par \uc2  Fund inflows were also expected to lend support to the won, after foreign investors bought a whopping net 671.6 billion won in local stocks on Thursday. Foreign investors have bought a net 7.95 billion won in stocks so far on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But local importers and interbank players continued to bid up the dollar, pushing the won just under the 1,300 level. That level is around the 50 percent retracement of the dollar's rise from February 21 to April 4. \par \par \uc2  Charts also showed the dollar/won was headed into oversold territory with a relative strength index reading of around 32. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  Political unrest and growing economic problems left the rupiah languishing beyond the 11,000 mark. \par \par \uc2  Local corporate dollar demand on Thursday forced interbank players to scramble for dollars in an illiquid market, shoving the rupiah past the key psychological level of 11,000. \par \par \uc2  Despite Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate hikes, analysts said the rupiah would continue to languish ahead of April 30, when parliament reconvenes and is expected to issue a second censure of the embattled President. \par \par \uc2  On the macroeconomic front, the World Bank warned that Indonesia's budget deficit may rise to 5-6 percent this year from a projected 3.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar reversed course overnight to hit lows of 1.8095 after solid performance in equities pushed it to highs of 1.7970 on Thursday from 1.8085. "People are not convinced by the rise in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (on Thursday)," a European bank dealer said. "Things may not be out of the woods." \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar has repaired less than a third of the damage caused by a steep four-percent fall to decade lows of 1.8230 on April 2 from the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, others expected it to show resilience in the face of 1.81 thanks to expectations for firmer stocks due to overnight gains on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  They added the reaffirmation of a tightening monetary stance by Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong on Thursday also discouraged bears to build massive long positions. \par \par \uc2  Lee said Singapore would maintain its policy of a gently appreciating band for the Sing dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said an immediate support for the Sing dollar lies at 1.8080-90 and a resistance at 1.7970. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR The Taiwan dollar was expected to trade nervously around 32.78-85 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to cut its rates by 0.125 percentage point following the Fed's surprise cut on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan last cut rates on March 29, lowering the discount rate to a historic low of 4.125 percent. \par \par \uc2  But a senior Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Thursday the bank would not immediately follow suit as it had to monitor the effects of the last rate cut on the domestic financial system and the economy. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aim in a rate cut would not be to inject liquidity into the market, but rather to bring about a slow depreciation of the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  But some argued a pro-growth market might think lower rates would help revive demand and boost the stock market, attracting further capital inflows and hence buoying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso was likely to stay under pressure from rising dollar demand by importers ahead of the import season but a steadier yen would provide some support on the downside. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary board on Thursday cut its key overnight rates by 50 basis points, matching the move a day earlier by the U.S. Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  The overnight borrowing rate now stands at 9.5 percent and the lending rate at 11.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  The rate cut would boost peso liquidity but dealers said the move was likely to soothe sentiment as monetary easing is needed to spur domestic consumption and investment. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht retreated against the U.S. dollar as euphoria surrounding the Fed's rate cuts on Wednesday was upstaged by strong dollar demand from importers and onshore companies. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said some oil companies had actively bid for dollars, taking advantage of easing oil prices and a steadier baht. They expected dollar profit taking to emerge around 45.50. \par \par \uc2  On the monetary front, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday kept its benchmark 14-day repo rate unchanged at 1.5 percent despite the Fed's interest rate reduction. \par \par \uc2  It also cut its forecast for economic growth this year to 2.5-4.0 percent from 3.0-4.5 percent in January, and trimmed core inflation expectations to 1.5-2.0 percent from 1.5-2.5 percent projected months ago. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0130 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     121.52\tab  121.69\tab    +0.14\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8080\tab  1.8025\tab    -0.30\par  Taiwan dlr    32.846\tab  32.899\tab    +0.16\par  Korean won   1298.00\tab 1294.00\tab    -0.31\par  Baht\tab      45.39\tab   45.23\tab    -0.35\par  Peso\tab      50.18\tab   50.05\tab    -0.26\par  Rupiah\tab 11140.00     11105.00\tab    -0.31\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct M