\par\par \b \uc2  Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  defends rates,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  policy. \b0\par\par\uc2 158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2001\par 10:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, April 20 (Reuters) - Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the ailing rupiah, which fell 3.4 percent earlier on Friday, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "could go anywhere" without it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and hiking interest rates, already at 21-month highs. \par \par \uc2  "Those are not useless. If Bank Indonesia did not do those things, the exchange rate could go anywhere ...," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sjahril Sabirin told reporters when asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and hiking interest rates were effective. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah extended its losses late on Friday, hitting lows of 11,500 in the face of active corporate dollar demand, dealers said. The rupiah lost 3.4 percent from its late Thursday levels, hitting a low of 11,500 per dollar, its weakest since September 14, 1998, according to Reuters data. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said it was driven down by local companies seeking dollars for imports and debt servicing amid expectations of further weakness in the rupiah. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4k01km8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
