\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets-Rally on U.S. rate cut, capped by concerns. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Nick Edwards \par \uc2 1,271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2001\par 12:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      HONG KONG, April 19 (Reuters) - Asian asset prices rallied \par \par \uc2  on Thursday, buoyed by a 50 basis point cut in U.S. interest \par \par \uc2  rates, but with upside capped by some suspicions that the \par \par \uc2  surprise move implied that the U.S. economy may be in worse \par \par \uc2  shape than feared. \par \par \uc2      "I think the markets are trying to work out whether this \par \par \uc2  cut is really good news or bad, that is, is there worse to \par \par \uc2  come?" Li Lian Ong, regional economist at Macquarie Bank in \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong said. \par \par \uc2      Retail investors, still counting their losses from the \par \par \uc2  region's 1997/98 economic crisis, were especially nervous. \par \par \uc2      "I had HK$600,000 invested in the stock market. Today it's \par \par \uc2  worth about $125,000. I have no more money to invest," said an \par \par \uc2  executive with a large container shipping company in Hong Kong, \par \par \uc2  who declined to be identified. \par \par \uc2  "For the average man on the street in Hong Kong, this rate \par \par \uc2  cut changes nothing. People are still poor, jobs are being lost \par \par \uc2  as industry relocates to China, and property prices are still \par \par \uc2  depressed," he said. \par \par \uc2  But economists said the U.S. Federal Reserve's cut - the \par \par \uc2  fourth in as many months that have brought key short term \par \par \uc2  borrowing rates down 200 basis points to 4.5 percent - was \par \par \uc2  positive for Asia. \par \par \uc2  "To the extent that this cut could help the U.S. economy \par \par \uc2  turn around faster, this is positive for the export oriented \par \par \uc2  economies of Asia," Ong told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Institutional investors followed that logic. \par \par \uc2  Stocks surged around the region with India's Bombay \par \par \uc2  benchmark index leading the charge, up around five percent in \par \par \uc2  late trade. Hong Kong's Hang Seng charged 4.4 percent higher. \par \par \uc2  Japan's bellwether Nikkei 225 chalked up a second \par \par \uc2  successive session of gains, hitting an intra-day high of \par \par \uc2  14,099.49 before easing back at the close, still 1.7 percent \par \par \uc2  higher and adding to the previous day's gain of 4.4 percent. \par \par \uc2      Singapore led the way in Southeast Asia, with the Straits \par \par \uc2  Times Index gaining 2.5 percent. \par \par \uc2      "It did offer a strong boost to local equities today...we \par \par \uc2  think that the Fed rate cuts do give central banks across the \par \par \uc2  region room to ease monetary policy further to bolster their \par \par \uc2  slowing economies," Paul Schymyck, an economist at financial \par \par \uc2  analysts IDEAglobal.com in Singapore, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2      But recent history does not offer much hope of a sustained \par \par \uc2  recovery in stock prices. \par \par \uc2      The Hang Seng, the Asian market most closely correlated to \par \par \uc2  the U.S., fell the morning after the last Fed rate cut on March \par \par \uc2  20 and had lost about two percent of its value since then \par \par \uc2  before Thursday's rally. \par \par \uc2  Singapore, heavily dependent on electronics exports to \par \par \uc2  trading partners including the U.S., has tracked a similar \par \par \uc2  path. \par \par \uc2      The STI dropped at the open on March 21 and had lost seven \par \par \uc2  percent in the interim before this rally. \par \par \uc2      And this is against a backdrop of very weak fund inflows. \par \par \uc2      In the first three months of this year, U.S. mutual fund \par \par \uc2  investors withdrew about $800 million from Asia and invested \par \par \uc2  only $300 million to create net outflows of about $500 million. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  CHEERED BY FED \par \par \uc2      Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were cheered by hopes that the Fed's cut \par \par \uc2  would prevent the growth engine of the world's biggest economy \par \par \uc2  from stalling. \par \par \uc2      A firmer yen and the Nasdaq's overnight rally also helped \par \par \uc2  regional units distance themselves from recent lows. \par \par \uc2      The yen poked above 122 to the dollar and the euro was firm \par \par \uc2  at three-week highs against the dollar as the market digested \par \par \uc2  the Fed's move. \par \par \uc2      Analysts said the Fed's decision to lower its federal funds \par \par \uc2  rate to 4.5 percent, the lowest since 4.25 percent in May 1994, \par \par \uc2  may help the regionals in the short term. \par \par \uc2      As expected, the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  matched the Fed's \par \par \uc2  50 basis-point rate cuts at its policy-setting meeting. \par \par \uc2      But Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would not immediately \par \par \uc2  follow the Fed's suit, while the Bank of Thailand was not \par \par \uc2  expected to immediately cut rates. \par \par \uc2      The rupiah bucked the firmer trend, suffering from a gloomy \par \par \uc2  political outlook. Despite Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it \par \par \uc2  slipped through the key psychological 11,000 mark. \par \par \uc2      BOND SPREADS NARROW \par \par \uc2      Asian dollar bonds sprang to life with selected sovereign \par \par \uc2  spreads 10-15 basis points tighter to U.S. Treasuries as the \par \par \uc2  Fed's rate cut lit a fire under U.S. equity markets and revived \par \par \uc2  interest in risk assets. \par \par \uc2  "The rate cut shows the Fed will take strong action to \par \par \uc2  revive demand, particularly capital spending. Since Asia is a \par \par \uc2  producing region, any revival in capital spending will benefit \par \par \uc2  this region," Raja Visweswaran, head of Asia securities \par \par \uc2  research at Bank of America in Hong Kong, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Korea's 8.875 percent sovereign bonds due in 2008 were \par \par \uc2  quoted some 15 bps tighter to Treasuries at 185 bps over. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian '09s were also quoted 15 bps tighter at 210 bps over. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian sovereign has been attracting bargain hunters \par \par \uc2  lately, Visweswaran said. It widened to 240 bps over Treasuries \par \par \uc2  a week and a half ago on expectations Kuala Lumpur may be \par \par \uc2  planning another sovereign bond issue. \par \par \uc2  "The view now is that any new issue is more likely to be a \par \par \uc2  euro issue or a yen-denominated issue which would offer a much \par \par \uc2  cheaper way to raise money than a dollar bond," Visweswaran \par \par \uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  Asian corporate bonds lagged sovereigns though, tightening \par \par \uc2  by about five bps over Treasuries across the board. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  1012 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     121.69\tab  122.95\tab    +1.04\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8025\tab  1.8054\tab    +0.16\par  Taiwan dlr     32.79\tab   32.92\tab    +0.41\par  Korean won   1294.00\tab 1313.60\tab    +1.51\par  Baht\tab      45.23\tab   45.37\tab    +0.31\par  Peso\tab      50.05\tab   50.10\tab    +0.10\par  Rupiah\tab 11105.00     10915.00\tab    -1.71\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7999\tab  3.7995\tab    -0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \p