\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Indonesian rupiah falls to 5-week lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2001\par 07:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 19 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah slipped through the 11,000 per dollar level for the first time in five weeks in the face of heavy corporate demand for dollars, dealers in Jakarta said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It fell to a low so far of 11,035 against 10,915 late on Wednesday, bringing its losses for the week to around four percent. By 0530 GMT, it was quoted at 11,000/020. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said companies and local banks actively bid for the dollar from 10,930 to settle import bills and to repay foreign debts due to fears the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could drop further. \par \par \uc2  "Political tensions have risen, with spats between President Abdurrahman Wahid and the parliament appearing to escalate," said a dealer at one European bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers added that with Bank Indonesia the sole supplier of dollars, the market was susceptible to panic dollar buying on any sign of local corporate dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia was spotted in the market when the rupiah slipped to 11,035, selling around $15-$20 million. The bank also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to shore up support for the ailing rupiah. \par \par \uc2  But dealers doubted whether the bank's persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would turn around the rupiah's fortunes in the face of \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unrelenting political and economic woes. \par \par \uc2  "Temporarily, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may help. But how long can they keep this up?" said a dealer at another European bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  "But they have no choice, or the rupiah would slide further." \par \par \uc2  In an effort to discourage further selling, Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to stem the rupiah's fall and raise rates on its benchmark certificates (SBIs). \par \par \uc2  The one-month SBI rate rose to 15.93 percent at Wednesday's auction from 15.82 percent the previous week. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have to reconsider its current policy given the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent weakness. \par \par \uc2  "Official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah has merely been able to stem the pace of the decline rather than stop it. High local interest rates have also failed to provide adequate support and are unlikely to do so in the near term," said Standard Chartered Bank in a research note. \par \par \uc2  The bank said the authorities faced tough policy decisions as a weaker rupiah would impact external debt and persistently high interest rates would make the government's budget deficit target tough to meet. \par \par \uc2  "Unfortunately, the current one is looking increasingly stale," it added. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah last breached 11,000 on March 12, when it hit a low of 11,210 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had limited ability to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4j01oa2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
